  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Warm and quiet weather today, clouds and more \nseasonable temperatures return for the weekend into early next week. \n\nA large surface high is ridging into the area from the Upper \nMidwest, while an upper-level ridge is across the Southeastern US. \nThis combination is keeping mostly clear skies and well above normal \ntemperatures across the area today. Highs are expected to reach into \nthe 70s for the mountains and into the low 80s for the Piedmont. A \ndry cold front will sweep through this afternoon and evening, \nwhich will keep breezy conditions in place with gusts up to 20 \nmph. Behind the front, temperatures will drop around 10-15 \ndegrees for tomorrow, returning to around normal for this time \nof year. Clouds will also increase late tomorrow afternoon, as \nan inverted trough forms along the Ohio River Valley. Halloween \nnight will be dry and seasonable, though heavy cloud cover and \nlight winds will add to the spookiness. \n\nHigh pressure will \"wedge\" in east of the Appalachians this\nweekend, but temperatures will still remain around normal due \nto the flow coming off of the Atlantic Ocean rather than from the\nNortheast. The clouds stick around through the weekend and into\nearly next week, with temperatures remaining slightly above \nnormal. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Next chance of rain will be mid to late next week.\n\nThe dry weather is expected to continue through at least \nTuesday, which would be the 17th consecutive day with no \nrainfall across most of the area. While that is nowhere near a \nrecord, it is noteworthy. The system that was expected to impact\nour area early next week is expected to remain off to our \nsouth. Rain chances slightly increase on Wednesday, with a better\nchance on Thursday. \n\nA large upper-level trough will dive down across the central \nCONUS, and move east into our area. Models are beginning to hint\nat the system weakening as it arrives, though confidence this \nfar out remains low. In addition, the synoptic setup could be \nquite complex, as there may be an additional offshore low \npressure system that could affect the approaching system from \nthe west. Regardless, mid to late next week is likely to be the\nbest chance of rain in the last three weeks. The system moves\nthrough and out of the area by next weekend.",
